   346	Sample preparation. The procedure described by Jolly et al. was adopted in preparing the samples for
   347	analysis, the latter via the technique of Energy Dispersive X-ray Fluorescence (EDXRF), in the Atmospheric and
   348	Environmental Chemistry Laboratory of the Atomic Energy Centre, ­Dhaka35. In specific terms, the preparation
   349	procedures consisted of cutting seaweed samples into small pieces, washed with tap water, and subsequently
   350	rinsed with deionized water, to then be left to dry at room temperature, followed by placement in an oven at
   351	60 °C until a constant dry weight was obtained. The dried seaweeds were then ground to obtain a fine homogenous powder using an agate mortar and pestle, with 0.1 g masses being pelletized using a hydraulic press pellet
   352	maker model (Specac Ltd., UK) by applying a pressure of approximately 3 tons. The dimension of the prepared
   353	pellets was 7 mm in diameter and 1 mm thick. This procedure was carried out for each triplicate of the 10
   354	seaweed species. The pellets were then stored in clean glass Petri dishes, then held in a vacuum desiccator for
   355	subsequent measurement of the concentrations of the various metals of concern.
   356	Sample analysis and method validation of the level of concern metals. The elemental concentration was analyzed by energy-dispersive X-ray fluorescence (EDXRF) spectroscopy, as described in our previous ­study36. In brief, a 109Cd point source with an X-ray beam (at 22.4 keV) was applied to excite the prepared
   357	samples and produce characteristic X-ray detected by the Si(Li) detector (Canberra™) which has a resolution of
   358	175 eV at 5.9 keV, amplified by the spectroscopy amplifier and processed by the multichannel analyzer MCA
   359	(6 K + channel). All the peak areas were integrated by AXIL and PRO/QXAS software provided by the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA), Vienna, Austria.
   360	With EDXRF a direct comparison method for elemental concentration measurement, calibration curves
   361	must be constructed based on similar matrices. Improving the sensitivity of readings and nullifying the matrix
   362	effects, the calibration curves were constructed via the use of the commercially available standard reference
   363	material (SRM) Orchard leaf/NIST 1571. The average peak areas of the EDXRF irradiated SRM pellets (at least
   364	three pellets) prepared using a similar configuration to that of the algae samples were then plotted in terms of the
   365	presence of the elements as a function of atomic ­number36. Validation of the calibration curve constructed for
   366	elements present in the standards was performed via analysis of another standard reference material, Spinach/
   367	NIST 1570a, again prepared in the same way as the algae ­sample35. All the obtained values were similar to certified values, the percentage relative error in evaluated elements being < 10%, assuring validation of the method.
   368	Comparison between the experimental and certified values is provided in Table 2.
   369	Samples were positioned in the EDXRF spectrometer according to the defined geometry and then excited
   370	using a 109Cd point source, providing 22.4 keV X-rays. To obtain good counting statistics, each sample was
   371	irradiated for a sufficient duration, ranging from 2000 to 5000 s. The collected spectra were analyzed using the
   372	aforementioned software, obtained from the IAEA. In this study, the standard addition method was used to
   373	obtain the metal concentration. The method involves the addition of known quantities of various analytes to
   374	the specimen. This method requires a linear calibration throughout the range of addition of various analytes. To
