   562	the significance of source-specific water
   563	management measures.
   564	Carcinogenic Health Risks
   565	The risk of cancer is the result of an average
   566	lifetime exposure to 1 mg/kg body
   567	weight/day of a pollutant. Incremental
   568	lifetime cancer risk (ILCR), was used to
   569	quantify cancer risk in this studies. ILCR
   570	below 1x10-6 are deemed inconsequential
   571	and can be ignored, but those above 1x10-4
   572	are deemed harmful, according to USEPA
   573	(1999), Eze et al. (2021), and Nawaz et al.
   574	(2023).
   575	
   576	Conversely, low risk of Pb was discovered
   577	in Borehole, Vendor, and Rainwater, while
   578	acceptable risk was found in the majority of
   579	sources for Pb in infants’ age group.
   580	However, while lead in tap, well, and sachet
   581	water is within an acceptable limit for
   582	infants, it poses a harmful risk to adults and
   583	children. This study's findings about the
   584	harmful hazards of Pb and Cd in certain
   585	water sources are in line with a study
   586	conducted in Nigeria by Etchie et al. (2018),
   587	which identified these metals as significant
   588	causes of cancer risk in urban water supplies.
   589	
   590	According to Table 5, which displays the
   591	ILCR of metals for adults, children, and
   592	infants in Gombe LGA, the highest cancer
   593	risk for all age groups is posed by chromium
