   660	Given that seaweed generally contains 80–90% ­water44, a median value of 85% was used for the calculations.
   661	An ordinary one-way analysis of the variance (ANOVA) was conducted to compare the concentrations of the
   662	13 observed elements among the 10 seaweed species, followed by Tukey’s multiple comparisons test. The same
   663	analyses were used to compare the concentration of the elements among types (Chlorophyta, Rhodophyta, and
   664	Phaeophyceae) by grouping seaweed species of the same color. Statistical significance was set to 0.05. All the
   665	analyses and graphs were performed in GraphPad Prism (version 8.4.3 for Windows).
   666	
   667	Results and discussion
   668	
   669	A total of 13 elements were determined and validated by the EDXRF technique for the 10 macroalgae species.
   670	Overall, the mean concentration of trace elements and heavy metals in the 10 species decreased in the descending
   671	order K > Fe > Zr > Br > Sr > Zn > Mn > Rb > Cu > As > Pb > Cr > Co (Table 5). The mean elemental concentration
   672	and descriptive statistics per macroalgae species are displayed in Table S1. As expected, K provides the greatest
   673	concentration among the 10 species (mean of 4.2 × ­104 mg ­kg−1), being significantly greater than the second most
   674	prevalent element (Fe, with a mean of 1.9 × ­103 mg ­kg−1). Previous studies have shown the prevalence of K to be
   675	in the same order. For instance, in Ulva spp. (Ireland), Chondrus crispus (Denmark), and Fucus spiralis (Spain),
   676	the mean K respective concentrations were 1.2 × ­104, 3.3 × ­104, and 4.0 × ­104 mg ­kg−145–47. Seaweeds naturally
   677	contain high K content, at of the order of 2% of their dry ­weight48,49, depending on the species and environmental
   678	conditions. Co concentrations have been found to be the lowest in value, at a mean of 0.26 mg ­kg−1 (ranging from
   679	0.18 to 0.33 mg ­kg−1 across the species); lower Co content has been reported in commercial seaweed of Asian
   680	origin (mean of 0.10 mg ­kg−1) and European origin (mean of 0.03 mg ­kg−1)50, constituting a naturally occurring
   681	micronutrient in ­seaweed51. Previous studies in aqueous solutions have demonstrated the leaching of elements
   682	such as Ca and Mg from grasses/algae, directly measured via inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry
   683	(ICP-MS)52. In the present study, macroalgae samples were not analyzed in aqueous solutions, thus leaching and
   684	loss of metallic elements from the samples being unlikely to have occurred.
   685	One-way ANOVA analysis shows a significant difference in the concentration of elements among macroalgae species (p =  < 0.05), except for Co (p = 0.3669) (Fig. 2a). Regarding the determined hazardous heavy
   686	metals, Pb, As, and Cr; the highest mean concentrations of Pb were found in B6 (10.63 mg ­kg−1), followed
   687	by R4 (4.50 mg ­kg−1), and B5 (4.24 mg ­kg−1) (Fig. 2b). For arsenic, two species, B5 (11.89 mg ­kg−1) and B6
