   701	(10.75 mg ­kg−1) had significantly higher concentrations than the rest (Fig. 2c). Lastly, R4 (3.64 mg ­kg−1), R3
   702	(1.94 mg ­kg−1), and B6 (1.90 mg ­kg−1) presented the highest levels of Cr (Fig. 2d). Interestingly, macroalgae
   703	species from the same genus (Hypnea; R1 and R2) showed similar bioaccumulation of heavy metals. B5 and B7
   704	(both genius Padina), showed considerable differences. This suggests that despite similar mechanisms of uptake,
   705	total nutrient/metal uptake may be species-specific for some seaweed genera.
   706	Despite being consumed as food or utilized as animal feed, there is no current legislation in Bangladesh that
   707	determines the maximum levels of heavy metals in seaweed. However, some international norms are available. Pb
   708	concentration in B6 surpassed the maximum levels in seaweeds (5 mg ­kg−1) recommended by the French High
   709	Council for Public ­Health53 and The Center for the Study and Development of Algae (CEVA)28. Moreover, the
   710	maximum levels of Pb in leafy vegetables (which may be consumed at similar levels to that of seaweed), according to the ­FAO54, is much lower (at 0.3 mg ­kg−1). To the best of our knowledge, maximum As and Cr levels in
   711	seaweed have not been addressed in international regulations. It should be noted that Sargassum sp. along with
   712	four additional species in the present study (R2, R4, G8, and G9) have been cultured and consumed in fresh or
   713	dried form for decades in ­Bangladesh32, thus presenting a potential route for heavy metal exposure to the population. Nevertheless, seaweed cultivation in Cox’s Bazar remains artisanal and ­undeveloped31.
   714	The concentrations of the three potentially toxic heavy metals that have been considered herein, Cr, As,
   715	and Pb, have been found comparable or lower than literature data elsewhere (Table 6). For instance, the mean
   716	concentration of Pb in 12 algal species in China was 1.89 mg ­kg−1 (ranging from 0.77 to 4.21 mg ­kg−1)24. In
   717	Lebanon, concentrations were even lower, with a mean of 1.04 mg ­kg−155. Cr and As were particularly high in
   718	Greece and China, with mean concentrations of 9.38 and 18.33 mg ­kg−1 ­respectively1,24,56. Conversely, much lower
   719	concentrations were reported from a market survey in ­Italy50. The mean concentrations of Cr, As, and Pb were
   720	0.14, 1.42, and 0.13 mg ­kg−1, respectively. This may be attributed to the high hygiene standards through the food
   721	supply chain and also during seaweed culture intended for human consumption. In markets from the Canary
   722	Islands (of Spain) reported Pb concentrations in seaweeds of Asian and European origin have ranged from 0.12
   723	to 0.004 mg ­kg−1 and from < LOQ to 0.05 mg ­kg−1, ­respectively57. The importation of potentially contaminated
   724	edibles as a source of heavy metal exposure has been discussed by others, including in regard to the consumption
   725	of seaweed. Particular examples include toxic industrial discharges in India, with toxic heavy metal pollution in
   726	areas of plant cultivation being a particular ­consequence58. A recent study carried out along the Palk Bay coast of
   727	southeast India has observed highly elevated Pb concentrations in many seaweed species, surpassing 10 mg ­kg−1
   728	in many cases. The variation in concentrations was found to depend on the sampling season. The main sources
   729	of contamination have been linked to ship washing activities, seafood processing, domestic sewage, and effluent
