   405	3.4. Analysis of the Main Components
   406	Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a statistical technique used to summarize the information content in
   407	large data tables by creating a smaller set of "summary indices" or principal components (Mahapatra, et. al, 2012,
   408	Grolemond and Wichham, 2017; Dalgaard, 2008). These components are easier to visualize and analyze than
   409	the original data. The PCA involves the diagonalization of the inertia matrix, and the eigenvalues obtained from
   410	this process correspond to the amount of variance intercepted by the factorial axes.
   411	In PCA, it is select the largest eigenvalues, denoted as λi, to determine the number of factorial axes required
   412	to establish a reduced dimension subspace. The choosing axes with the maximum inertia is akin to constructing
   413	new variables associated with the axes of maximum variance. The principal axes of inertia, also known as
   414	direction axes, are the eigenvectors of the matrix of variances-covariance, which are normalized to decide on the
   415	number of axes to retain in PCA, several methods can be used, such as:
   416	1. Scree Plot: This plot displays the eigenvalues against the corresponding principal components. The point
   417	where the plot levels off or "flattens" indicates the optimal number of components to retain.
   418	2. Kaiser Criterion: According to this criterion, only eigenvalues greater than 1 should be retained as they
   419	explain more variance than a single original variable.
   420	3. Modified Kaiser Criterion: This criterion is an adjusted version of the Kaiser criterion and helps in
   421	determining the number of components to keep based on the eigenvalues.
   422	4. Anderson Criterion: The Anderson criterion uses a statistical test to determine the number of components to
   423	retain.
   424	By applying these criteria, it could be decided how many principal components to be considered in the PCA,
   425	allowing for a reduced dimension representation of the original data while retaining the most significant variance.
